        "id": 1025452
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-06T15:57:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The statement is right and it goes along to what was originally before (see [diff](https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/c/gerrit/+/579081/2/polygerrit-ui/app/elements/change/gr-change-actions/gr-change-actions.ts)) IOW\n\nIt was:\n```\nif (this.change?.can_ai_review \u003d\u003d\u003d false) {\n```\n\nwhich means `true` only when `can_ai_review` is exactly `false` — i.e. `false` (body skipped) if `change` is `undefined`/`null`, or `can_ai_review` is `undefined` or `true`.\n\nthere is:\n```\nif (this.revisionActions?.aiReview?.enabled \u003d\u003d\u003d false) {\n```\n\nwhich means `true` only when `enabled` is exactly `false` — i.e. `false` if `revisionActions` is `undefined`/`null`, or `aiReview` is `undefined`/`null`, or `enabled` is `undefined` or `true`.\n\nBoth flags are typed `boolean | undefined` in TS, so the value space is bounded — the guard fires in exactly one state, which makes the two checks behaviorally equivalent.\n\nIOW it works like this:\n```\nconst testing1 \u003d {};\nconst testing2 \u003d {\n  foo: {\n    name: \"some\"\n  }\n};\nconst testing3 \u003d {\n  foo: {\n    bar: false\n  }\n};\n\nconsole.log(`testing1 evaluates to ${testing1.foo?.bar !\u003d\u003d false}`);\nconsole.log(`testing2 evaluates to ${testing2.foo?.bar !\u003d\u003d false}`);\nconsole.log(`testing3 evaluates to ${testing3.foo?.bar !\u003d\u003d false}`);\n```\n\nwhich produces the following:\n```\nReset\n\"testing1 evaluates to true\"\n\"testing2 evaluates to true\"\n\"testing3 evaluates to false\"\n```\n\nThat blocks AI review button ONLY when action is explicitly disabled (set to `false`).\n\nRefs:\n- [Optional chaining `?.`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Optional_chaining) — short-circuits to `undefined` on a nullish step.\n- [Strict equality `\u003d\u003d\u003d`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Strict_equality) — no type coercion.",
